1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:04,150
Hi, I'm Edwin, student number 25092450.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:06,000
Today I'll talk about seafloor spreading rates.

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:12,050
The seafloor spreading rate usually means how fast the plates on either side of a mid-ocean ridge move apart.

4
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:14,700
It can be split into the half-spreading rate and the full-spreading rate.

5
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:18,250
With symmetric spreading, the full rate is exactly twice the half rate.

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:20,300
So how do we measure it?

7
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:21,500
This is the mid-ocean ridge.

8
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:23,700
The two paper strips are the seafloor on either side of it.

9
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,900
When I pull the strips outward, new paper comes out from here,

10
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:29,600
just like new oceanic crust forming at the ridge.

11
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:33,900
As the rock cools, it records the direction of Earth's magnetic field at that time.

12
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:38,050
The magnetic patterns on the two strips are perfectly symmetric.

13
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:43,100
Now suppose this stripe is 100 km from the ridge and 5 million years old.

14
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:45,900
Then the half-spreading rate is 100 km ÷ 5 million years,

15
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,500
which is 2 cm per year.
